  "generated_text": "Given that \\(\\gcd(a,b) = 168\\) and \\(\\gcd(a,c) = 693\\), we need to find the smallest possible value of \\(\\gcd(b,c)\\).\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 168 and 693:\n\\[\n168 = 2^4 \\times 7\n\\]\n\\[\n693 = 3 \\times 11^2\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(a,b) = 168\\) and \\(\\gcd(a,c) = 693\\), \\(a\\) must be a common factor of both 168 and 693. Therefore, \\(a\\) must include the prime factors \\(3\\) and \\(7\\).\n\nTo find the smallest possible value of \\(\\gcd(b,c)\\), we need to consider the common factors between \\(b\\) and \\(c\\). The common factors of 168 and 693 are \\(3\\) and \\(7\\). The smallest of these common factors is \\(3\\).\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\(\\gcd(b, c)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[3, '3']",
    "gold_answer": "Note that $\\gcd(168,693) = 21$.  Since $\\gcd(a,b) = 168 = 8 \\cdot 21$, both $a$ and $b$ are divisible by 21.  Since $\\gcd(a,c) = 693 = 21 \\cdot 33$, both $a$ and $c$ are divisible by 21.  Therefore, $\\gcd(b,c)$ must be at least 21.\n\nIf we take $a = 5544$ (which is $21 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 33$), $b = 168$, and $c = 693$, then $\\gcd(a,b) = \\gcd(5544,168) = 168$, $\\gcd(a,c) = \\gcd(5544,693) = 693$, and $\\gcd(b,c) = \\gcd(168,693) = 21$, which shows that the value of 21 is attainable.  Therefore, the smallest possible value of $\\gcd(b,c)$ is $\\boxed{21}$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
